
## Defining features

A feature is a named condition that rules can use. You define it once, in the language below, and it is computed for you on every instance from the model{% if definitions.general %}, so it means the same everywhere: use the named constants, and only the numbers 0 and 1{% endif %}.

- Labels of the model, in double quotes:{% for name, text in definitions.labels %} `"{{ name }}"` (`{{ text }}`){% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if definitions.training %} (as on instance 1; the values differ on each instance){% endif %}.
- The state variables and constants above, `true`, `false`, numbers, `=`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `+`, `-`, `&`, `|`, `!`, `=>` and parentheses.
- `exists v: P` and `forall v: P`: P holds for some / for every value of the state variable `v` (put the whole quantifier in parentheses).
- `next(P)`: the action's most likely outcome satisfies P.
- `slip(P)`: some other possible outcome of the action satisfies P.
- `stays(v, w)`: the action's most likely outcome leaves the variables `v`, `w` unchanged.
- `old(v)`, only inside `next(...)` or `slip(...)`: the value of `v` before the move.
{% if definitions.level >= 1 %}
- `closer_to(T, avoid A)`: the action's most likely outcome is strictly closer to a state where T holds, counting most-likely moves along a shortest way that never passes through a state where A holds (`, avoid A` is optional).
{% endif %}
{% if definitions.level >= 2 %}
- `progress_to(T, avoid A)`: the expected number of steps to reach T, counting every possible outcome and a large extra cost for each step in a state where A holds, is lower after the action.
{% endif %}

A definition that uses `next`, `slip`, `stays`{% if definitions.level >= 1 %}, `closer_to`{% endif %}{% if definitions.level >= 2 %} or `progress_to`{% endif %} is a per-action feature: in a rule it is evaluated for the rule's action. A definition can also give one condition per action: `{"definition": {{ '{' }}{% for a in actions %}"{{ a }}": "..."{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{{ '}' }}}`.

Every definition comes with 2 to 4 `checks`: example states{% if definitions.training %} of instance 1{% endif %}, each written as a condition (any numbers allowed), with the action for a per-action feature, and whether the feature should hold there. At least one check must expect `true` and one `false`. Each check is tested on every reachable state the condition describes; a definition whose checks disagree with what it computes is returned to you with those states, so you can fix the definition or the check.

Example definitions for a different problem (a corridor of cells pos = 0..5 with a pit at 2 and an exit at 5, where a move succeeds with probability 0.8 and slips one cell back otherwise; not this one):
```
{"name": "pit_ahead", "description": "the move's likely outcome is the pit", "definition": "next(\"pit\")", "checks": [{"state": "pos = 1", "action": "right", "holds": true}, {"state": "pos = 1", "action": "left", "holds": false}]}
{"name": "blocked", "description": "the move does not change the position", "definition": "stays(pos)", "checks": [{"state": "pos = 0", "action": "left", "holds": true}, {"state": "pos = 0", "action": "right", "holds": false}]}
{"name": "pit_slip", "description": "a slip of the move could end in the pit", "definition": "slip(\"pit\" & pos != old(pos))", "checks": [{"state": "pos = 3", "action": "right", "holds": true}, {"state": "pos = 4", "action": "right", "holds": false}]}
{% if definitions.level >= 1 %}
{"name": "toward_exit", "description": "the move gets nearer the exit without crossing the pit", "definition": "closer_to(\"exit\", avoid \"pit\")", "checks": [{"state": "pos = 3", "action": "right", "holds": true}, {"state": "pos = 1", "action": "right", "holds": false}]}
{% endif %}
```
